Since we have $\sin(2x)$ in our expression, let's rewrite it using its double angle identity $\sin(2x)=2\sin(x)\cos(x)$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\sin(x)}{\sin(2x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sin(x)}{2\sin(x)\cos(x)}\qquad\gray{\sin(2x)\text{ identity}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\sin(x)}}{2\cos(x)\cancel{(\sin(x))}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{2\cos(x)}\text{, for }x\neq \{...,-2\pi, -\pi, 0, \pi, 2\pi, ...\}
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x $-values (in their domains) except for $k\pi$ for any integer $k$, and specifically $0$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
